// hw-si — generated from src/hw-si-*.js by tools/merge.js. Edit the src files, then run: node tools/merge.js
// Signal Integrity Engineer. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["hw-si"] = (QB["hw-si"] || []).concat([
["Role overview", "What does a signal integrity engineer do?", "**Why they ask:** the opening question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I make sure fast signals arrive clean enough for the receiver to read them correctly. Before the board is built, I define stack-up and routing rules, then simulate critical channels — memory buses, PCIe, Ethernet, SerDes links — to check eye diagrams and margins. After the board is built, I measure with high-bandwidth scopes, VNAs and TDRs, and correlate measurements back to simulation. Often I cover power integrity too, since noisy power hurts signals.\"\n\nTip: Show both pre-layout simulation and post-layout measurement.", "B", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Which tools do you use?","Tools like HyperLynx, ADS or Ansys SIwave for simulation, and a VNA, TDR and high-speed scope in the lab."]}],
["Fundamentals", "What causes reflections, and how do you control them?", "**Why they ask:** the core concept.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Reflections happen wherever the impedance changes along a path — at vias, connectors, stubs, or a mismatched termination. Part of the signal bounces back and adds ringing. I control them by keeping impedance continuous: controlled-impedance traces, optimized via designs with back-drilling to remove stubs, good connector transitions, and correct termination at the right end. TDR measurements show exactly where the discontinuities are.\"\n\nTip: 'Any impedance change reflects' is the key idea.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What is back-drilling?","Drilling away the unused part of a through-hole via, which removes the stub that resonates and hurts high-speed signals."]}],
["Crosstalk", "How do you reduce crosstalk?", "**Why they ask:** dense boards suffer from coupling.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Crosstalk comes from coupling between nearby traces, so spacing is the main tool — a common rule is three times the trace-to-plane height between aggressive signals. Keeping traces close to a solid reference plane reduces coupling. I minimize parallel run length, route adjacent layers at right angles, and use stripline for the most sensitive signals. Then I simulate the worst-case aggressor patterns to confirm the margin.\"\n\nTip: Mention the 3H spacing rule as a starting point, not a law.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's the difference between near-end and far-end crosstalk?","Near-end shows up at the end close to the driver; far-end at the receiver end — far-end is mostly absent in stripline."]}],
["S-parameters", "Explain S-parameters and how you use them.", "**Why they ask:** the language of high-speed channels.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"S-parameters describe how a channel transmits and reflects energy across frequency. For a channel, insertion loss — S21 — shows how much signal gets through, and return loss — S11 — shows reflections. I extract them from models or measure with a VNA, then check them against the standard's limits, like the PCIe compliance masks, and feed them into channel simulations to predict the eye.\"\n\nTip: Tie S21 and S11 to loss and reflection.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What makes insertion loss worse at higher frequencies?","Conductor loss from skin effect and dielectric loss in the board material both increase with frequency."]}],
["Eye diagram", "What does an eye diagram tell you?", "**Why they ask:** how you judge link quality.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"It overlays many bit periods so you can see the signal's quality at a glance. Eye height shows voltage margin and eye width shows timing margin. Closure comes from loss, reflections, crosstalk, jitter and noise. I compare the eye against the receiver's mask from the standard, and if it's closed I look at equalization settings, channel loss, or the specific impairment that's closing it.\"\n\nTip: Link each kind of closure to a cause.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What is jitter?","Variation in when the signal edges arrive compared to their ideal time, which eats into timing margin."]}],
["Equalization", "How does equalization help a lossy channel?", "**Why they ask:** modern SerDes depend on it.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A lossy channel attenuates high frequencies more than low, smearing bits into each other. The transmitter can pre-emphasize — boosting transitions with FFE. The receiver uses CTLE to boost high frequencies and DFE to cancel remaining inter-symbol interference from previous bits. In simulation I sweep settings to find the best combination, then confirm with the chip's on-die eye scan.\"\n\nTip: Name FFE, CTLE and DFE and where each sits.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why can't equalization fix everything?","It also amplifies noise and crosstalk, and DFE can't remove reflections that are far from the current bit."]}],
["Power integrity", "What is power integrity and why does it matter for SI?", "**Why they ask:** PI and SI are linked.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Power integrity means keeping the supply voltage at each chip steady during fast current changes. If the power delivery network's impedance is too high, the voltage droops and ripples, which adds jitter and noise to signals. I design the PDN to a target impedance across frequency using planes, decoupling capacitors chosen for different frequency ranges, and short connections, then simulate and measure it.\"\n\nTip: 'Target impedance' is the key phrase.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["How do you calculate target impedance?","Allowed voltage ripple divided by the expected transient current."]}],
["Vias", "How do vias affect high-speed signals?", "**Why they ask:** vias are often the biggest discontinuity.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A via is a short impedance discontinuity, and the unused stub below the exit layer acts like a resonant antenna that can notch out frequencies. For fast links I minimize layer changes, back-drill or use blind vias to remove stubs, tune the antipad size to match impedance, and place ground return vias right next to signal vias.\"\n\nTip: Stubs and return vias are the main points.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["At what data rates do via stubs become a problem?","Usually from around 10 Gbps upward, depending on stub length and board thickness."]}],
["Correlation", "How do you correlate simulation with measurement?", "**Why they ask:** a credible SI engineer trusts measured data.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I start with test coupons and structures on the board to measure material properties and trace loss, then update the simulation models with the real dielectric constant and loss. I compare TDR impedance profiles and S-parameters between simulation and measurement. When they agree within a few percent, I trust the model for future designs. When they don't, it usually points to fabrication differences or a modeling simplification.\"\n\nTip: Show you improve models with real data.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What causes most mismatches?","Different real material properties, copper roughness, and fabrication tolerances on trace width and dielectric thickness."]}],
["DDR", "What are the key challenges in DDR memory routing?", "**Why they ask:** almost every processor board has DDR.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"DDR is a parallel bus with tight timing, so length matching within byte lanes matters, along with strobe-to-data skew. Address and command lines use fly-by topology with termination at the end. I watch crosstalk because many signals run close together, and power integrity because many outputs switch at once. I follow the memory controller's guidelines and simulate the worst-case byte lane.\"\n\nTip: Mention fly-by topology and byte-lane matching.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What is write leveling?","A DDR training step where the controller adjusts timing per byte to make up for the different flight times in fly-by routing."]}],
["Behavioral", "Tell me about a high-speed problem you solved.", "**Why they ask:** real experience.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A 25 Gbps link had a high error rate on one lane only. TDR showed an impedance dip at a connector footprint. The pads were too wide, and the reference plane under them was solid, which lowered impedance. We added a cut-out in the plane under the pads, simulated it, and the next revision passed with good margin. Since then we simulate every connector footprint for links above 10 Gbps.\"\n\nTip: Show measurement-driven root cause and a process change.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["Why did only one lane fail?","Its routing had a slightly longer path and extra via, so it had less margin left to absorb the connector discontinuity."]}],
["Closing", "Why do you want this signal integrity role?", "**Why they ask:** motivation and fit.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Signal integrity is where physics meets product, and getting a fast link to work first time is very satisfying. Your next platform moves to 112 Gbps links, which is exactly where careful modeling and correlation matter most. I'd bring a strong simulation-to-lab process and like to help set the design rules the whole team uses.\"\n\nTip: Show you want to shape team standards.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["How do you keep up with new standards?","Follow standards bodies like PCI-SIG and IEEE, vendor app notes, and conferences like DesignCon."]}]
]);

