QB["hw-si"] = (QB["hw-si"] || []).concat([
["Transmission lines", "When does a PCB trace start behaving like a transmission line?", "**Why they ask:** it's the core SI judgment call.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"It's about the signal's rise time versus the trace's electrical length, not the clock frequency. The rule of thumb is that if the trace is longer than roughly a sixth of the rise-time length — the distance the edge travels during its rise — you have to treat it as a transmission line and worry about reflections. A signal travels about 15cm per nanosecond on FR-4, so a 1ns edge has a rise length around 15cm, and anything beyond a few centimeters needs controlled impedance and termination. Fast edges, not high clocks, drive SI.\"\n\nTip: Rise time sets the knee frequency — a 1ns edge has significant content past 350MHz regardless of the clock.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What's the knee frequency?","Roughly 0.35 divided by the rise time — the frequency above which the edge has little energy; you design the channel to pass up to it."]}],
["Impedance", "How is trace impedance controlled in a stackup?", "**Why they ask:** controlled impedance is foundational.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Characteristic impedance comes from the trace width, the dielectric thickness to the reference plane, the dielectric constant, and the trace thickness. To hit a target like 50 ohms single-ended or 100 ohms differential, I work with the fab's stackup: for a given dielectric height and Dk, I solve for the width with a field solver or the fab's calculator. I specify the target impedance and tolerance, usually plus or minus 10%, on the fab drawing, and the fab adjusts and can coupon-test it. Keeping the reference plane continuous under the trace is as important as the geometry.\"\n\nTip: Width, dielectric height, Dk and copper thickness set impedance — and the fab must hit a stated tolerance.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why specify an impedance tolerance to the fab?","Because real dielectric and etch vary; a stated tolerance like plus or minus 10% tells the fab to measure and adjust, often with a test coupon."]}],
["Termination", "Compare series, parallel and AC termination.", "**Why they ask:** picking the right termination is everyday SI.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Series termination puts a resistor near the driver matching the line impedance minus the driver output — it absorbs the reflection coming back and suits point-to-point nets, costing no static power. Parallel termination puts a resistor to a reference at the far end to match the line — great for multi-drop or incident-wave signaling, but it draws DC current. Thevenin termination is two resistors to the rails, setting a bias and impedance, used on some buses. AC termination adds a series cap so there's no DC current but still damping. I choose by topology, power budget and signaling scheme.\"\n\nTip: Series = source, no static power, point-to-point; parallel = far end, costs power, good for multi-drop.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Where do you place a series termination resistor?","As close to the driver as possible, so the whole trace past it sees the matched source impedance."]}],
["Return path", "Why does the return path matter for a high-speed signal?", "**Why they ask:** return path is the most common SI mistake.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"High-frequency return current flows on the reference plane directly under the trace, because that path has the lowest loop inductance, not necessarily the shortest copper. If that plane has a gap, a split, or the trace changes reference layers without a nearby return via, the return current has to detour — which increases loop area, adds inductance, radiates EMI, and causes crosstalk and ground bounce. So I keep a solid, continuous reference plane under every high-speed trace and add stitching vias where a signal changes layers. 'Mind the return current' is half of SI.\"\n\nTip: Return current hugs the trace on the reference plane — never route high-speed signals over a plane gap.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What happens if a trace crosses a plane split?","The return current must detour around the gap, creating a large loop that radiates and couples noise — a classic EMI and SI failure."]}],
["Differential pairs", "What rules do you follow routing a differential pair?", "**Why they ask:** diff pairs carry most high-speed interfaces.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I keep the two traces tightly and consistently coupled with constant spacing to hold the differential impedance, usually 100 ohms, and route them together over a continuous reference. I match their lengths closely to control skew — intra-pair skew turns differential signal into common mode. I keep them away from aggressors, symmetric through bends and vias, and I add length-matching where one leg is longer, correcting near where the mismatch happens rather than at the end. Symmetry is the whole point — anything that breaks it between the two legs hurts.\"\n\nTip: Tight, symmetric coupling and matched length — skew between the legs becomes common-mode noise.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why is intra-pair skew bad?","It converts differential signal into common-mode, which radiates as EMI and degrades the receiver's ability to reject noise."]}],
["Skew", "How do you manage length matching and skew on a parallel bus?", "**Why they ask:** timing closure on buses like DDR.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Each bit has to arrive within the setup-and-hold window, so I match trace lengths within a group — a byte lane and its strobe for DDR — to a tight tolerance, often a few mils, since about 150ps corresponds to roughly an inch on FR-4. I use serpentine tuning to add length to short traces, but I keep the serpentines loosely coupled so they don't self-couple, and I match to the strobe or clock, not just to each other. For source-synchronous buses, matching data to its strobe matters more than absolute length.\"\n\nTip: Match to the clock/strobe; about 150ps per inch is the mental conversion.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why match data to the strobe rather than absolute length?","Source-synchronous buses clock data with the strobe, so what matters is their relative timing, not the absolute delay down the board."]}],
["Rise time", "Why is edge rate more important than clock frequency in SI?", "**Why they ask:** a fundamental misconception to clear up.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A signal's high-frequency content comes from how fast its edges switch, not how often. A slow 100MHz clock with very sharp 100ps edges has energy well into the gigahertz and will reflect, cross-talk and radiate, while a 100MHz signal with soft edges may be benign. So when I assess a net I look at the rise time, compute the knee frequency, and design for that bandwidth. It's also why slowing an edge, where timing allows, is a cheap fix for EMI and reflections — you cut the high-frequency content.\"\n\nTip: Fast edges, not fast clocks, create SI problems — and slowing edges is a real fix.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["When can't you just slow the edge?","When timing needs the fast edge for setup/hold margin, or the device driver doesn't offer slew control — then you must terminate instead."]}],
["Decoupling", "How do you choose and place decoupling capacitors?", "**Why they ask:** decoupling ties SI and power integrity together.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Decoupling caps supply the transient current a chip draws when it switches, so the local voltage doesn't sag. I use a spread of values so different caps are effective at different frequencies, place the smallest, highest-frequency ones closest to the power pins with the shortest, lowest-inductance connection — vias right at the pad — because the loop inductance, not just capacitance, sets how well they work at high frequency. Bulk caps handle lower-frequency demand. The goal is low power-delivery-network impedance across the frequencies the chip switches at.\"\n\nTip: Placement and via inductance matter as much as the capacitor value at high frequency.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why does the smallest cap go closest to the pin?","Its benefit is at high frequency where connection inductance dominates, so it must have the shortest, lowest-inductance path to the pin."]}],
["TDR", "What is TDR and what do you learn from it?", "**Why they ask:** a key SI measurement tool.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Time-domain reflectometry sends a fast step down the line and watches the reflections come back. Because the reflection's sign and size depend on impedance changes, I can read the impedance profile along the trace versus distance — a bump up means a higher-impedance spot like a necked-down trace or a connector, a dip means lower impedance like a via or a fat pad. It's how I locate exactly where an impedance discontinuity is, verify a controlled-impedance trace, and debug a connector or via launch. TDR shows you where, not just that, there's a problem.\"\n\nTip: TDR maps impedance versus position — it localizes the discontinuity.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["How do you convert TDR time to position?","Using the signal's propagation velocity on that medium — time times velocity, halved for the round trip — gives the distance to the discontinuity."]}],
["Loss mechanisms", "Where does loss come from in a high-speed channel?", "**Why they ask:** understanding loss drives material and length choices.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Two main mechanisms. Conductor loss rises with the square root of frequency because of skin effect — current crowds to the surface, so the effective resistance grows. Dielectric loss rises roughly linearly with frequency and depends on the material's loss tangent, Df. At multi-gigabit rates dielectric loss usually dominates, which is why low-loss materials matter for long, fast channels. There's also radiation and loss from roughness on the copper. I budget total insertion loss across the channel and choose material, trace width and length to stay within what equalization can recover.\"\n\nTip: Conductor loss goes as root-f, dielectric loss as f — dielectric dominates at high speed.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What is loss tangent (Df)?","A material property measuring how much energy the dielectric dissipates per cycle; lower Df means a lower-loss, better high-speed laminate."]}],
["Skin effect", "Explain skin effect and why it matters.", "**Why they ask:** fundamental high-frequency behavior.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"At high frequency, current doesn't flow uniformly through a conductor — it crowds into a thin layer at the surface, the skin depth, which shrinks as frequency rises. So the effective cross-section carrying current shrinks and the AC resistance goes up, roughly with the square root of frequency. That's why conductor loss increases with frequency, why surface roughness matters more at high speed, and why you can't assume DC resistance for a gigahertz signal. It pushes designers toward smoother copper and wider traces for long high-speed links.\"\n\nTip: Current crowds to the surface; AC resistance rises as root-f, so copper roughness matters.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why does copper roughness matter at high frequency?","Because current flows in a thin surface layer, a rough surface lengthens the effective path and adds loss — profile-controlled copper helps."]}],
["Jitter", "What are the main types of jitter and why distinguish them?", "**Why they ask:** jitter budgeting is central to serial links.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I split jitter into random and deterministic. Random jitter is unbounded, Gaussian, usually from thermal and phase noise, so you describe it statistically and it sets the BER floor. Deterministic jitter is bounded and has a cause: inter-symbol interference from channel loss, duty-cycle distortion, periodic jitter from a coupled clock, and crosstalk. Separating them matters because you fix them differently — equalization attacks ISI, better shielding attacks crosstalk, a cleaner PLL attacks random and periodic jitter. Total jitter at a target BER combines both.\"\n\nTip: Random is unbounded/statistical (sets BER); deterministic is bounded with a fixable root cause.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What is ISI jitter?","Inter-symbol interference — a lossy channel smears each bit into the next, shifting edges; it's the deterministic jitter equalization targets."]}],
["BER", "What is a bathtub curve and how do you use it?", "**Why they ask:** serial link margin analysis.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A bathtub curve plots bit error rate versus sampling position across the unit interval — errors are high near the edges where the eye closes and drop to a floor in the middle where it's open. The width of the low-error bottom at a target BER, like 1e-12, is your timing margin — how much the sampling point can move before errors climb. I use it to quantify eye opening at a realistic BER instead of eyeballing a scope eye, and to see whether margin is limited by random jitter (steep walls) or deterministic jitter (wide walls).\"\n\nTip: The flat bottom width at the target BER is the real timing margin.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why not just read a scope eye diagram?","A scope eye shows a limited number of bits; a bathtub extrapolates to very low BER like 1e-12, which you can't capture directly in reasonable time."]}],
["Equalizers", "Compare FFE, CTLE and DFE.", "**Why they ask:** modern SerDes use all three.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"They all fight channel loss and ISI. FFE — feed-forward equalization, often as transmit de-emphasis — pre-distorts the signal so the channel flattens it out; it's simple but amplifies crosstalk. CTLE, a continuous-time linear equalizer at the receiver, boosts high frequencies to offset loss, cheap and low-power but it also boosts noise. DFE, a decision-feedback equalizer, uses already-decided bits to cancel their trailing ISI — powerful and doesn't amplify noise, but can't fix pre-cursor ISI and can propagate a wrong decision. Real links combine a CTLE plus DFE, with TX FFE, and auto-adapt the settings.\"\n\nTip: CTLE boosts (and amplifies noise), DFE cancels post-cursor ISI without boosting noise, FFE pre-distorts at the TX.", "D", "X", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why can't a DFE fix pre-cursor ISI?","It works from already-decided past bits, so it can only cancel ISI that trails an edge, not energy that arrives before the main bit."]}],
["Via design", "How do you design a high-speed via or back-drill a stub?", "**Why they ask:** vias are a top discontinuity at multi-gigabit.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A via is a small impedance discontinuity: the barrel, pads and antipads add capacitance and inductance, and any unused barrel length below the signal layer is a stub that resonates and sucks energy out at a frequency set by its length. I keep antipads sized to tune impedance, place a return via right next to a signal via so the return current follows, minimize pad and stub, and for long stubs on thick boards I back-drill — mechanically remove the unused barrel — to kill the stub resonance. Optimizing the via launch is often worth more than the trace itself at high speed.\"\n\nTip: Return via next to the signal via, and back-drill stubs on thick boards to remove resonances.", "D", "X", null, {"f":["Why is a via stub bad?","The unused barrel acts like a resonant antenna that creates a notch in the channel response at a frequency set by its length."]}],
["Ground bounce", "What is simultaneous switching noise (ground bounce)?", "**Why they ask:** a classic multi-output problem.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"When many outputs on a chip switch at once, they all draw current through the shared power and ground inductance of the package and planes, and that di/dt across the inductance creates a voltage glitch — the chip's internal ground bounces relative to the board. It can corrupt quiet signals, cause false switching, and shows up as noise on the rails. I fight it with good decoupling right at the package, low-inductance power delivery, spreading output timing, controlling edge rates, and enough ground pins and vias. It couples PI and SI directly.\"\n\nTip: Many outputs switching through shared inductance causes a di/dt voltage glitch — fix with low-inductance PDN and decoupling.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why does edge rate affect SSN?","Faster edges mean higher di/dt, and the noise voltage is inductance times di/dt, so sharper edges make ground bounce worse."]}],
["Materials", "How do you pick a laminate material for a high-speed design?", "**Why they ask:** material choice drives cost and performance.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I look at the loss tangent Df and the dielectric constant Dk and its stability over frequency, plus copper roughness and cost. Standard FR-4 is cheap but lossy and has variable Dk, fine for moderate speeds. For long multi-gigabit links I move to low-loss laminates with controlled Dk and smooth copper to keep insertion loss and skew manageable. I also consider the glass-weave effect — Dk varies with where the trace sits over the weave, causing skew — so for sensitive diff pairs I may spec a tighter weave or route at an angle. It's a cost-versus-margin trade.\"\n\nTip: Df for loss, Dk stability for impedance and skew, copper roughness for high-frequency loss.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's the fiber-weave effect?","Dk varies between glass bundles and resin, so two legs of a pair can see different velocity and build skew; angled routing or tighter weave reduces it."]}],
["IBIS", "What is an IBIS model and when do you need one?", "**Why they ask:** models drive pre-layout simulation.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"IBIS is a behavioral model of a chip's I/O — it describes the driver and receiver with voltage-current and voltage-time tables and package parasitics, without exposing the internal circuit, so vendors share it freely. I use IBIS for pre-layout and signal-integrity simulation of reflections, ringing and timing on a net. For multi-gigabit SerDes with equalization I need IBIS-AMI, which adds algorithmic models of the TX and RX equalizers so the simulator can run millions of bits and produce an accurate eye and bathtub. Picking the right model fidelity is part of the job.\"\n\nTip: Plain IBIS for reflections and ringing; IBIS-AMI when equalization and statistical eye analysis are involved.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why IBIS-AMI instead of plain IBIS for SerDes?","AMI models the TX/RX equalizers algorithmically, letting the tool simulate millions of bits for a statistical eye — plain IBIS can't represent adaptive EQ."]}],
["Pre vs post layout", "What's the difference between pre-layout and post-layout SI analysis?", "**Why they ask:** SI work spans the whole design flow.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Pre-layout, before the board is routed, I simulate topologies, termination options, stackup and length limits to set the rules the layout must follow — it's about making decisions and writing constraints. Post-layout, I extract the actual routed geometry, often with a 3D or 2.5D field solver, and verify the real channel meets the eye, loss and crosstalk targets, catching problems the ideal pre-layout model missed. Pre-layout prevents problems cheaply; post-layout confirms and finds the surprises. Skipping pre-layout means you find issues when they're expensive to fix.\"\n\nTip: Pre-layout sets constraints and prevents problems; post-layout verifies the real routed channel.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why not just do post-layout once?","Because fixing a topology or stackup problem after routing is slow and costly; pre-layout sets the rules so the layout is right the first time."]}]
]);

// Signal Integrity Engineer. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["hw-si"] = (QB["hw-si"] || []).concat([
["VNA measurement", "How do you measure a channel's S-parameters with a VNA?", "**Why they ask:** hands-on SI characterization.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I connect the vector network analyzer to the channel's ports, calibrate first to move the reference plane to the probe tips — SOLT with cal standards, or TRL for fixtures — and then sweep frequency, capturing insertion loss, return loss and crosstalk as S-parameters. The calibration is the part people get wrong; without it the cables and fixtures are in your result. For a board without connectors I use probes or a test coupon. Then I de-embed the fixture so what I report is the channel itself, and I compare it against the simulation.\"\n\nTip: Calibration and de-embedding decide whether you're measuring the channel or your own cables.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What's the difference between SOLT and TRL calibration?","SOLT uses known short/open/load/thru standards; TRL uses transmission-line standards and is better for on-board fixtures where ideal lumped standards are hard to make."]}],
["Insertion loss budget", "How do you build an insertion-loss budget for a serial link?", "**Why they ask:** channel budgeting is a senior SI skill.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I add up the loss contributors between the TX and RX at the Nyquist frequency: the two packages, each board trace, the vias, connectors and any cable. The spec for the standard gives a maximum channel loss the receiver's equalization can recover, so I check my total stays under it with margin. If it's over, I shorten traces, use lower-loss material, improve via launches, or push loss into the equalization budget knowingly. It turns 'is this routable' into a quantified decision before layout.\"\n\nTip: Sum loss at Nyquist and keep it under what the RX equalization can recover, with margin.", "D", "X", null, {"f":["At what frequency do you evaluate the loss?","At the Nyquist frequency — half the bit rate — since that's where the fundamental of an alternating bit pattern sits and loss hurts most."]}],
["Compliance", "What does compliance testing for an interface like PCIe or USB involve?", "**Why they ask:** real products must pass compliance.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Each standard defines tests the link must pass — a transmitter eye measured against a mask at a reference point, often after a defined reference channel and equalizer, jitter decomposition, and return-loss limits, plus receiver tolerance where you stress the input and confirm it still hits the BER. I set up the scope or BERT with the standard's compliance software, use the specified test fixtures and patterns, and measure against the masks. The goal is interoperability — passing compliance means it should work with any other compliant device.\"\n\nTip: Compliance = TX eye/mask, jitter breakdown, return loss, and RX stress tolerance, per the spec's fixtures.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why measure the TX eye after a reference channel?","Because the spec defines performance at the receiver end; applying a reference channel and equalizer models what a real RX would see."]}],
["Crosstalk types", "What's the difference between NEXT and FEXT?", "**Why they ask:** crosstalk analysis detail.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Both are coupling from an aggressor into a victim. Near-end crosstalk, NEXT, appears at the end of the victim nearest the aggressor's driver and travels back toward it; it's driven mainly by capacitive and inductive coupling and shows up quickly. Far-end crosstalk, FEXT, appears at the far end and depends on the difference between capacitive and inductive coupling and the coupled length — it can grow with how long the lines run parallel. They matter differently by topology: NEXT hurts bidirectional and nearby receivers, FEXT hurts the far receiver on long parallel runs. Spacing, shorter parallel length and a good reference reduce both.\"\n\nTip: NEXT comes back toward the source end; FEXT arrives at the far end and grows with coupled length.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Which is worse for a long parallel bus?","FEXT, because it accumulates with the coupled length, so long parallel runs especially need spacing or a guard."]}],
["Guard traces", "Do guard traces actually help, and when?", "**Why they ask:** a common misconception.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A guard trace only helps if it's properly grounded with stitching vias along its length, roughly every tenth of a wavelength, so it stays at ground potential and gives return current a nearby path. A floating guard trace does little and can even make things worse by coupling. Often the better money is spent on more spacing — the 3W rule — and a solid reference plane, which usually beats a guard trace for the effort. So my answer is: guards work when well-stitched and the space is tight, but spacing and a clean reference are the first tools.\"\n\nTip: An unstitched guard trace is useless or harmful; spacing plus a solid plane usually wins.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's the 3W rule?","Keep center-to-center spacing of about three trace widths between a sensitive signal and its neighbor to cut coupling to a small level."]}],
["AC coupling", "Where do AC-coupling capacitors go in a SerDes link and why?", "**Why they ask:** placement affects the eye.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Most high-speed serial links are AC-coupled with series capacitors so the two ends can have different common-mode voltages. The caps are a discontinuity, so I place them symmetrically on both legs of the pair, usually on the transmit side near the connector, with pads optimized to minimize the impedance bump — small-body parts and reference-plane cutouts under the pads if needed. Matched placement keeps the pair symmetric; a mismatch adds skew and common-mode. The value is chosen so the high-pass corner is well below the signal's low-frequency content.\"\n\nTip: Symmetric placement on both legs and optimized pads keep the AC caps from closing the eye.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why does the AC cap value matter?","It sets a high-pass corner; too small a cap and long runs of same-value bits droop (baseline wander), so the value must pass the signal's lowest content."]}],
["Spread spectrum", "What is spread-spectrum clocking and what's the SI trade-off?", "**Why they ask:** SSC is common for EMI but affects links.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Spread-spectrum clocking slightly modulates the clock frequency over time so its energy spreads across a band instead of sitting in a sharp spike, which lowers the peak EMI the product radiates and helps it pass emissions limits. The trade-off is that the data rate now wanders, so the receiver's clock-recovery loop has to track it, and downstream clocks that must stay synchronous need to handle the modulation. For most serial links the CDR tolerates the standard's SSC profile, but you have to confirm the budget includes it.\"\n\nTip: SSC trades a lower EMI peak for added tracking burden on clock recovery.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why does SSC reduce measured EMI?","It spreads the clock's energy over a frequency band, lowering the peak amplitude at any single frequency where the limit is measured."]}],
["PDN impedance", "What is a power delivery network target impedance?", "**Why they ask:** PI underpins SI.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Target impedance is the maximum impedance the power delivery network can have across frequency and still keep the rail ripple within tolerance for the chip's transient current. You estimate it as the allowed ripple voltage divided by the transient current step. Then I design the PDN — plane capacitance, decoupling caps of various values, and the VRM — so the impedance stays below that target from DC up to where the on-die capacitance takes over. Peaks in PDN impedance, from anti-resonances between caps and planes, are where rails misbehave, so I flatten those.\"\n\nTip: Target Z = allowed ripple ÷ transient current; keep PDN impedance below it across frequency.", "D", "X", null, {"f":["What's an anti-resonance in a PDN?","A peak in impedance where a capacitor's inductance resonates with another capacitance, creating a frequency where the rail is poorly decoupled."]}],
["Setup and hold", "How do you build a timing budget for a parallel source-synchronous bus?", "**Why they ask:** connecting SI to timing closure.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I account for every term between driver and receiver: clock-to-output of the driver, flight time down each trace, the setup and hold the receiver needs, clock skew, jitter, and the effect of ISI and crosstalk on the edges. The data must arrive and be stable within the receiver's setup-and-hold window relative to the strobe across all corners. SI feeds this directly — crosstalk and reflections eat into the margin, so a clean channel buys timing margin. If the budget doesn't close, I tighten length matching, reduce loading, or slow the interface.\"\n\nTip: SI margin is timing margin — crosstalk and ringing directly consume setup/hold.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["How does crosstalk steal timing margin?","It shifts and distorts the victim's edges, effectively moving the crossing point, which narrows the window where data is valid at the receiver."]}],
["Reference change", "What happens when a signal changes reference planes, and how do you fix it?", "**Why they ask:** layer transitions are a frequent SI bug.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"When a trace vias from referencing one plane to another, the return current has to find a way from the first plane to the second. If those planes are at the same potential, a nearby stitching via gives the return a low-inductance path. If they're different nets — say signal referenced to ground then to a power plane — the return must jump through decoupling caps or plane capacitance, a long detour that adds inductance, noise and EMI. So I add a stitching via right next to the signal via when planes share a net, or add a nearby stitching cap when they don't, and I avoid unnecessary reference changes.\"\n\nTip: Put a return/stitching via next to every signal via that changes reference layers.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What if the two reference planes are different nets?","Place a stitching capacitor nearby to give the high-frequency return a path, since a via alone can't connect two different-net planes."]}],
["Connectors", "Why are connectors often the weakest link in a high-speed channel?", "**Why they ask:** real channels include connectors.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A connector forces the signal through a 3D structure — pins, the mating interface, and the via launches on both boards — that's hard to keep at constant impedance, so it adds reflection, crosstalk between neighboring pins, and loss. At multi-gigabit rates the connector and its launch footprint can dominate the channel's return loss. I model the connector with the vendor's S-parameters, optimize the breakout and via launch on each side, assign ground pins to shield sensitive pairs, and pick a connector rated for the data rate. The launch design on the PCB is often where I recover the most margin.\"\n\nTip: Optimize the PCB launch footprint and use the vendor S-parameters — the launch often matters more than the connector itself.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why do ground-pin assignments in a connector matter?","Grounds next to signal pairs provide return paths and shielding between pairs, cutting crosstalk through the connector."]}],
["Eye closure", "A link's eye is closing. How do you diagnose the cause?", "**Why they ask:** structured SI debugging.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I separate the contributors. If the eye closes mostly vertically, it's loss or amplitude — check channel insertion loss and whether equalization is set right. If it closes horizontally, it's jitter — then I break down the jitter: ISI points to channel loss or reflections, periodic jitter to a coupled clock or power noise, crosstalk to a nearby aggressor. I use TDR to find reflections, look at the PDN for power-induced jitter, and check equalizer settings. Measuring, then attributing the closure to a mechanism, tells me which fix — EQ, shielding, termination, or layout — to apply.\"\n\nTip: Vertical closure = loss/amplitude; horizontal closure = jitter — then decompose the jitter to find the cause.", "D", "X", null, {"t":1,"f":["Eye closes only when a neighboring lane is active — what is it?","Crosstalk from that aggressor lane; increase spacing, improve the reference, or check the connector/via coupling between them."]}],
["EMI link", "How are signal integrity and EMI related?", "**Why they ask:** SI engineers often own EMI too.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"They share root causes. The same fast edges and discontinuities that hurt SI also radiate: a signal crossing a plane gap makes a big current loop that both corrupts the signal and radiates efficiently as an antenna. Common-mode currents from skew or an imbalanced pair radiate strongly. So many EMI fixes are SI fixes — solid reference planes, controlled return paths, matched differential pairs, slower edges where allowed, and good decoupling. I think of EMI as SI's return current escaping where it shouldn't. Fixing SI properly usually improves emissions.\"\n\nTip: Broken return paths and common-mode current hurt SI and radiate — the fixes overlap.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why does common-mode current radiate so well?","It flows in the same direction on both conductors with no nearby return to cancel it, so even a small common-mode current radiates efficiently."]}],
["Simulation fidelity", "How do you decide how detailed an SI simulation needs to be?", "**Why they ask:** over- or under-modeling both cost you.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I match the tool to the question. For reflections and ringing on a moderate-speed net, a 2D field solver with IBIS and a transmission-line model is fast and enough. For multi-gigabit channels with vias, connectors and plane effects, I need a 3D full-wave solver to extract accurate S-parameters, then channel simulation with IBIS-AMI for the statistical eye. Over-modeling wastes days; under-modeling misses the via or connector that actually breaks the link. I validate the model against a measurement when the stakes are high, and only add fidelity where it changes the answer.\"\n\nTip: Use the simplest model that captures the dominant effect; escalate to 3D only where vias, connectors or planes matter.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["When is a 2D solver not enough?","When the geometry is truly 3D — vias, connectors, plane cutouts — where current flows in ways a 2D cross-section can't capture."]}],
["Behavioral", "Tell me about a time your simulation disagreed with measurement.", "**Why they ask:** correlation and humility matter in SI.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A serial link simulated with good margin but measured a closed eye on the bench. I didn't just trust the sim — I went back and found the model assumed an ideal via, while the real board had a long via stub on a thick stackup that I'd modeled as negligible. Re-extracting with the actual stub showed a resonance notch right near Nyquist that matched the measurement. We back-drilled the stubs and the eye opened. It taught me to model the via launch realistically and always correlate before trusting a clean sim.\"\n\nTip: A concrete correlation story — the via stub — shows real SI judgment and humility.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What did you change in your process after that?","I started extracting real via and launch geometry in post-layout and correlating against a TDR/VNA measurement on the first build."]}],
["Scenario", "A new board revision intermittently fails a high-speed interface in the field. Where do you start?", "**Why they ask:** real-world SI problem solving.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Intermittent and field-dependent points to marginal SI plus environment — temperature, voltage, or unit-to-unit variation eating thin margin. I compare the revision's changes to the last good one — a stackup, material, connector, or routing change can quietly cut margin. I measure the channel on failing and passing units with a VNA and look at the eye and bathtub across temperature and corner. If margin is thin, I find the biggest contributor — often a via, connector or a reference discontinuity introduced in the rev — and recover margin there, or adjust equalizer settings. I confirm with margin testing across the full range, not just room conditions.\"\n\nTip: Intermittent field failure = thin margin; diff the revision, then measure the eye across temperature and corners.", "D", "X", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why test across temperature and voltage, not just at the desk?","Because loss, timing and device behavior shift with conditions; a link that's marginal only passes at the bench but fails hot or at low voltage in the field."]}]
]);

// Signal Integrity Engineer. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["hw-si"] = (QB["hw-si"] || []).concat([
["Microstrip vs stripline", "When do you route high-speed signals as microstrip versus stripline?", "**Why they ask:** layer choice is a basic SI decision.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Microstrip runs on an outer layer with one reference plane and air above, so it's lower loss per length, easy to probe, and faster propagation, but it radiates more and is exposed to crosstalk and environment. Stripline is buried between two planes, so it's shielded — lower EMI and crosstalk — and has a controlled, symmetric reference, at the cost of higher loss from being fully in dielectric and no easy access. I use microstrip for short, accessible, high-speed escapes and stripline for sensitive signals that need shielding and tight impedance control over longer runs.\"\n\nTip: Microstrip = lower loss, exposed, probe-able; stripline = shielded, controlled, buried.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why does microstrip propagate faster than stripline?","Part of its field is in air, which has a lower effective dielectric constant than fully-embedded stripline, so the signal travels faster."]}],
["Diff pair geometry", "Edge-coupled versus broadside-coupled differential pairs — how do you choose?", "**Why they ask:** layout geometry detail.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Edge-coupled puts the two traces side by side on the same layer — it's the default, easy to route and length-match, and keeps the pair symmetric to one reference. Broadside-coupled stacks them on adjacent layers, which can save space and give tight coupling, but it's hard to keep symmetric: the two layers can have different impedance and the vias and layer transitions break symmetry, adding skew. I use edge-coupled almost always and only consider broadside when routing density forces it, knowing I'll fight skew and asymmetry.\"\n\nTip: Edge-coupled is the symmetric default; broadside saves space but risks skew and asymmetry.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's the main risk with broadside pairs?","The two legs on different layers see different environments and via structures, so they build skew and impedance mismatch that's hard to correct."]}],
["Clock jitter", "How does a reference clock's phase noise affect a serial link?", "**Why they ask:** clock quality feeds link performance.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The transmitter and receiver clocks come from PLLs locked to a reference, and the reference's phase noise plus the PLL's own noise become jitter on the recovered clock and the data edges. High close-in phase noise shows up as low-frequency jitter the clock-recovery loop can track; high-frequency phase noise above the loop bandwidth passes straight through and closes the eye horizontally. So a noisy reference or a poorly designed PLL eats timing margin directly. I budget clock jitter alongside channel ISI, and for demanding links I spec a low-phase-noise reference and clean its supply.\"\n\nTip: Phase noise becomes jitter; what's above the CDR loop bandwidth passes through and closes the eye.", "D", "X", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why can the CDR track low-frequency jitter but not high-frequency?","The clock-recovery loop follows slow wander within its bandwidth, but jitter faster than the loop can respond passes through to the sampling point."]}],
["Baseline wander", "What is baseline wander and how do you avoid it?", "**Why they ask:** an AC-coupling subtlety.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"In an AC-coupled link, a long run of the same bit value has low-frequency content that the coupling capacitor's high-pass response droops, so the signal's baseline drifts and the eye closes — that's baseline wander. It's avoided by encoding the data so it's DC-balanced — line codes like 8b/10b or scrambling limit how many identical bits run in a row — and by sizing the AC caps so the high-pass corner is well below the lowest content the code allows. So the fix is partly coding and partly choosing the right coupling capacitor.\"\n\nTip: DC-balanced coding plus a low-enough high-pass corner prevents the baseline from drifting.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why does 8b/10b coding help?","It bounds run length and keeps the data DC-balanced, so there's little low-frequency content for the AC caps to droop."]}],
["Serpentine", "What's the risk with serpentine (trombone) length-tuning, and how do you route it well?", "**Why they ask:** a practical layout pitfall.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Serpentine tuning adds length to match delay, but if the back-and-forth segments run too close, the trace couples to itself — energy shortcuts across the loops, which actually reduces the effective delay you were trying to add and distorts the signal. So I keep the parallel segments spaced at least a few trace widths apart, keep the serpentine amplitude reasonable, and place the tuning where the mismatch occurs rather than dumping it all at one end. For diff pairs I tune symmetrically so I don't create skew while fixing length.\"\n\nTip: Space the serpentine segments enough to avoid self-coupling, or the added length won't deliver the delay you expect.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why does tight serpentine coupling reduce the intended delay?","The signal couples across adjacent segments and takes a shortcut, so the effective electrical length is less than the physical copper you added."]}]
]);
